{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Ben Oni</b> — son of my suffering. \"Benjamin\" — it appears to me that since he alone was born in the land of Canaan, which is in the south when one comes from Aram Naharaim, as it says, \"in the south, in the land of Canaan\" (Numbers 33:40), \"going on still toward the south\" (Genesis 12:9) — Benjamin [means] son of the right [hand], in the language of \"north and south You created them\" (Psalms 89:13), and therefore it is spelled plene [with a ו], in the language of Rashi. But I do not understand this, that the land of Israel should be south of Aram Naharaim, for Aram is east of the land of Israel, as it is written (Genesis 29:1) \"and he went to the land of the people of the east,\" and it is written (Numbers 23:7) \"from Aram, Balak king of Moab brought me, from the mountains of the east,\" and Jacob crossed the Jordan, which is to the east of the land of Israel, and he was returning by way of Edom, which is in the south of the land of Israel; it turns out that Aram is southeast of the land of Israel, and the land of Israel is to its north. But if he was born in the vicinity of Bethlehem Ephrathah, which is in the land of Judah, as it is written (Judges 19:2) \"Bethlehem in Judah,\" and it is written (Micah 5:1) \"and you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, too small to be among the thousands of Judah\" — then it is in the south of the land of Israel, and he was born between Bethel and Bethlehem Ephrath. And if the place is in the hill country of Ephraim, then it is in the north of the land of Israel, as it is written (Joshua 18:5) \"Judah shall stand by their border on the south, and the house of Joseph shall stand by their border on the north.\" And if it was in the portion of Benjamin, it is also not in the south, as it is written, \"and their border was on the north side.\" In any case there is no reason to call him \"son of the south.\" \n\nAnd the correct interpretation in my eyes is that his mother called him Ben Oni, intending to say \"son of my mourning,\" from \"bread of mourning\" (Hosea 9:4), \"I have not eaten of it in my mourning\" (Deuteronomy 26:14). But his father interpreted Oni as \"my strength,\" from the language of \"the first of my strength\" (Genesis 49:3), \"and to him who lacks strength\" (Isaiah 40:29). Therefore he called him Benjamin — son of strength or son of might, for in the right hand lies valor and success, as in the verse (Ecclesiastes 10:2) \"a wise man's heart is at his right hand,\" (Psalms 21:9) \"Your right hand shall find out those who hate You,\" (Psalms 118:16) \"the right hand of HaShem is exalted.\" He wished to call him by the same name his mother had called him, for all his sons are called by the names their mothers called them, and he translated it into a sense of good and valor.\n\nAnd I saw in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 72:9): \"Ben Oni\" — son of my suffering; \"and his father called him Benjamin\" — in the holy tongue. And I do not know what this means, for everything is in the holy tongue, and so the names of all his sons are in the holy tongue. Rather, they alluded to what I said, that he translated the expression into one of good:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The closing colon (:) after לטובה appears to mark the end of the comment with a trailing gesture toward what was already explained, not an ellipsis; rendered as is."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:04.771223515Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}